[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107178,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetus in fetu (FIF) is an exceptional developmental anomaly in which a parasitic fetus is contained within the body of its twin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107179,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107180,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107181,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> internal parasitic twin; external parasitic twin; epignathus; fetiform teratoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107182,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107183,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FIF is most commonly intra-abdominal (70&ndash;80% of cases) {24689738; 31175400}, although it may occur in many different locations, including the gonads [[Landmann A, Calisto JL, Reyes-M&uacute;gica M, et al. Fetus-in-fetu presenting as a cryptorchid testis and abdominal mass: a report of a case and review of the literature. J Pediatr Surg Case Rep. 2016 Oct;13:38&ndash;40. doi:10.1016/j.epsc.2016.07.010.]], sacrum, oropharynx, orbit, intracranial structures, and thorax {28949025; 29193240; 18447681; 21901374; 31413324}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107184,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FIF most commonly manifests as an asymptomatic abdominal mass and is usually discovered by prenatal ultrasound {11762548}. The original term is attributed to Johann Friedrich Meckel, circa&nbsp;1800 {13761753}. It is currently considered a type&nbsp;0 germ cell tumour {31413324}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107185,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The estimated incidence is of FIF is 1&nbsp;case per 500&nbsp;000&nbsp;live births {24689738}. Most occur in neonates or young children, but rare cases in adults have been reported {31409619}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107186,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genetic data point towards an abnormality in the development of monozygotic/diamniotic twins.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107187,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>More than 15% of FIF cases have a family history of twinning. It is currently thought that blastomeres in the two-cell (2C) stage, which are defined as omnipotent, are prone to escaping the organizing influence of the developing embryo or their developmental potential, leading to FIF {31413324}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107188,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>By definition, FIF appears as a tumour mass with variable fetiform features. Some reach a strikingly fetus-like stage of development, including mobility of the limbs {11762548} and the development of other recognizable portions of a fetal body. FIF should be distinguished from mature/fetiform teratoma by the presence of metameric segmentation, demonstrated radiologically by evidence of vertebral organization.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107189,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histopathology of FIF is similar to that of mature teratomas but with a higher degree of organization. Full bowel and multiple organs are commonly recognized.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107190,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107191,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most analyses reveal genotypic identity between the host and the FIF {18447681}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107192,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> advanced organoid development; evidence of axialization and metameric segmentation (vertebral organization).</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> enclosure by a distinct sac; partial or complete normal skin coverage; grossly recognizable anatomical parts; attachment to the host by only a few relatively large blood vessels; immediate adjacency to one of the sites of attachment of conjoined twins, or association with the neural tube or gastrointestinal system.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107193,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107194,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most are cured with simple surgical resection. There are reports of recurrences with a malignant component, especially yolk sac tumour {9349774}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-08T15:41:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]